  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.1607.05994},
  urldate = {2024-04-21},
  abstract = {Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) and Geometric Edit Distance (GED) are basic similarity measures between curves or general temporal sequences (e.g., time series) that are represented as sequences of points in some metric space \$(X, \textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft dist\textbraceright )\$. The DTW and GED measures are massively used in various fields of computer science, computational biology, and engineering. Consequently, the tasks of computing these measures are among the core problems in P. Despite extensive efforts to find more efficient algorithms, the best-known algorithms for computing the DTW or GED between two sequences of points in \$X = \textbackslash mathbb\textbraceleft R\textbraceright\textasciicircum d\$ are long-standing dynamic programming algorithms that require quadratic runtime, even for the one-dimensional case \$d = 1\$, which is perhaps one of the most used in practice. In this paper, we break the nearly 50 years old quadratic time bound for computing DTW or GED between two sequences of \$n\$ points in \$\textbackslash mathbb\textbraceleft R\textbraceright\$, by presenting deterministic algorithms that run in \$O\textbackslash left( n\textasciicircum 2 / \textbackslash log\textbackslash log n \textbackslash right)\$ time. Our algorithms can be extended to work also for higher dimensional spaces \$\textbackslash mathbb\textbraceleft R\textbraceright\textasciicircum d\$, for any constant \$d\$, when the underlying distance-metric \$\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft dist\textbraceright\$ is polyhedral (e.g., \$L\_1, L\_\textbackslash infty\$).},
